 same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Del Rio has set <strong>46 standing record lows</strong>, fewer than the 81 that chance alone would give, and <strong class=\"sig\">208 record highs</strong>, far more than the 81 that chance alone would give. The 2020s hold the most record highs of any decade (125). Del Rio's all-time high is 115\u00b0F, on June 21, 2023.","t_chg":"Del Rio's days and nights both warmed. Days warmed faster.","p_chg":"Across 120 complete years: Del Rio's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.17\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.10 to +0.24), about +2.0\u00b0F since 1906. Del Rio's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.34\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.26 to +0.43), about +4.1\u00b0F since 1906.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 9 of 12 months. Highs rose in 11 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Del Rio's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.00 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 19.8 inches a year. The wettest year was 1914 (37.75 in); the driest, 1956 (4.34 in). Del Rio almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.1 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":true},"t_year":"2026 so far: 5 daily record highs, 1 daily record low"}}